WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:05.230
Any self-respecting Pixar fan

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:07.700
could tell you that the
animation company's movies

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:08.950
are filled with references

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:11.370
and Easter eggs of its back catalog.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:13.810
But producer Mark Nielsen has
admitted that there's nods

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:17.500
to every single Pixar
movie in "Toy Story 4."

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.025
We're gonna be exploring them in 3, 2, 1.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:28.900
As usual, we will be
issuing a spoiler alert

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:30.260
because we're about to be revealing

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.480
some details from the film.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:35.350
The movie opens with a
flashback to nine years ago,

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:38.750
explaining the absence of
Bo Peep from "Toy Story 3."

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:41.090
She was last spotted
seen with Woody, Buzz,

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.930
Jessie, and Wheezy in "Toy Story 2,"

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:45.700
but fans complained that it wasn't clear

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:49.270
whether she'd been broken or was donated.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:54.140
Outside, the license plate
on the car is RMR F97.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:56.170
License plates have been used in "Cars"

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:59.810
and "Toy Story 2" to mean
a whole range of things.

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:01.980
This one refers to when "Toy Story 2"

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.592
was accidentally deleted
from Pixar's edit system.

00:01:05.592 --> 00:01:07.980
RMR F97 was the command

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.150
on the computer that deleted the film,

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:12.663
which had to be restored
using a backup file.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:17.700
Bo Peep assists Woody in
rescuing RC from a storm drain.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.000
A card is passed through the
group to use as a launchpad.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.827
On this card is a crude
drawing of Rainbow Unicorn

00:01:23.827 --> 00:01:24.868
from "Inside Out."
Joy: I loved you in

00:01:24.868 --> 00:01:26.300
"Fairy Dream Adventure Part Seven."

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:27.133
OK, bye.

00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:28.937
Woody: Are a toy!

00:01:28.937 --> 00:01:30.580
You belong to Bonnie!

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:32.420
Shardlow: Take a closer look at the boxes

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:35.770
at the back of the classroom
when Bonnie creates Forky.

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:38.690
One of them belongs to
Anton, a possible reference

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:42.250
to food critic Anton Ego in "Ratatouille."

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.120
Bonnie's room has some similarities

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:47.040
to the wallpaper in Andy's
room in "Toy Story."

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.190
And later in the film, we see a bedspread

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:50.980
full of Pizza Planet-style rockets

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.448
that are the same as
in the original movie.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.840
The van that the family
goes on vacation in

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.070
is a Tri-County RV.

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:01.300
This links to references

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:04.840
to Tri-County International
Airport in "Toy Story 2"

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.810
and Tri-County Landfill in "Toy Story 3."

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:10.160
One of the vacation spots

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.410
is a home that looks eerily similar

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:14.193
to the "Incredibles" house.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.960
Woody and Forky come across
Second Chance Antiques.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:20.430
The store sign tells us

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:24.233
that it was established in
1986, the same year as Pixar.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:29.280
Also, the building address is 1200.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:30.510
Now, Pixar is based

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:34.293
at 1200 Park Avenue,
Emeryville, California.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:39.180
Outside the antique store
is a Ship It removals van.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.703
This same company van crops
up in "Toy Story of Terror."

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:45.890
Inside the store is where we get lots

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:48.320
of movie references packed in.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:52.350
There's a vintage record from
Chalupa Records, from "Coco."

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:53.760
There's a green diver's mask

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:57.235
and tiki heads from the
fish tank in "Finding Nemo."

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:02.000
Bo Peep slides past a plate

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.920
that has an ornate-looking
symbol from "Brave" in it.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.143
This plate can be seen in several shots.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:10.260
A yellow plane hanging near the window

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.453
resembles Sunwing from "Planes."

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.320
There are also awards cases

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.510
from Gusteau's office in "Ratatouille"

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:20.320
and furniture from "The Incredibles."

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:22.050
Take a closer look at the key

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:25.020
that the antique-store
staff member is holding.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:27.470
It looks very much like Sora's Keyblade

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:28.994
from "Kingdom Hearts."

00:03:28.994 --> 00:03:30.760
Fans have speculated that this is a nod

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:34.119
to the upcoming Square Enix
title "Kingdom Hearts III."

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.460
The antagonists from the antique store

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:39.433
are a group of vintage toys.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.330
The ventriloquist dummy Vincent

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:45.490
bears a resemblance to Slappy
the Dummy from "Goosebumps,"

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:48.130
and Gabby Gabby has the
same yellow bows and dress

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:50.727
as Chatty Cathy from the 1960s.

00:03:53.449 --> 00:03:55.510
Here's where "The Shining" comes in.

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:58.050
From carpets to
garbage-truck license plates,

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:00.190
Stanley Kubrick's film
has made an appearance

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:02.480
in many "Toy Story" movies.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:06.290
A record is put on playing
"Midnight, the Stars and You,"

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:09.400
as performed by Ray
Noble and his orchestra.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.830
This is the same song as played
at the end of "The Shining,"

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:15.190
as the camera pans out from a photograph

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:18.650
showing Jack smiling at
the Overlook Hotel ball.

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:21.890
Fans have also suggested that
the old lady's home address

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:25.190
when attacked by Ducky and Bunny is 237.

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:27.250
So keep an eye out for that.

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:29.000
Combat Carl is back!

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:30.550
We see a Combat Carl doll

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:33.490
being blown up by Sid in
the first "Toy Story."

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:35.670
This version, though, is
a Carl Weathers lookalike

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:37.540
from "Toy Story of Terror."

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:39.060
Rumor has it that John Lasseter

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:42.350
wanted to use a G.I. Joe
doll in the original movie,

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:44.800
but Hasbro was upset that
the doll would be blown up,

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:47.580
so they agreed to make up a brand instead.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:50.320
Buzz flies into a
purple-and-yellow spiderlike ride

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.520
that has the same color palette as Zurg,

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:54.313
his enemy in "Toy Story 2."

00:04:56.649 --> 00:04:59.080
There's a spinning ride
next to the Ferris wheel

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:01.500
with a purple-and-yellow
umbrella structure.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:05.007
This looks exactly like the
sundrop flower from "Tangled."

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:10.910
A stall is called Jet Stream,

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:13.323
echoing the character
alias Strut Jetstream.

00:05:14.269 --> 00:05:15.636
Strut Jetstream: Man,
what's going on here!

00:05:15.636 --> 00:05:16.736
Is everybody getting to fly today?

00:05:19.168 --> 00:05:20.443
Shardlow: It's in this fairground

00:05:20.443 --> 00:05:23.090
that Bo Peep loses her arm.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:26.480
Throughout Disney-owned
franchises, characters lose an arm

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:29.530
in a nod to Luke
Skywalker's amputation scene

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:31.728
at the end of "The Empire Strikes Back."

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:35.570
Bo's sheep bring her a bottle cap

00:05:35.570 --> 00:05:37.910
and a safety pin while out foraging.

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:38.880
The cap they give her

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:41.605
is from the same
grape-soda bottle in "Up."

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:44.450
Later in the film,

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:47.180
Buzz gets trapped in a
ring-toss-style game.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.853
Other prizes include
the guitars from "Coco,"

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:53.023
rockets featuring the Pixar ball,

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:56.030
and a red-and-yellow stuffed toy flame,

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:58.173
resembling Anger in "Inside Out."

00:06:01.371 --> 00:06:03.460
Think Duke Caboom looks a bit familiar?

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:05.730
He actually cropped up in "Incredibles 2,"

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:07.906
lying in the corner of Jack-Jack's crib.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:11.810
Bo and Woody approach the secret entrance

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:13.950
for a club in the antique store.

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:17.280
Above this, we see a
decoration for Catmull's Brews,

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:20.270
after Ed Catmull, the
long-standing studio exec

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:23.580
who's stepping down after "Toy Story 4."

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:25.950
Bo presses a 25-cents button

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:28.360
on the slot machine to enter the club.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:31.490
The number 25 pops up a
few times in the film.

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:35.180
This could be a nod to the "Toy
Story 3" maker Lee Unkrich,

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:39.243
who's stepping down at Pixar
after 25 years in 2019.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:43.010
Inside the club, we meet Tin Toy

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:45.900
from the 1988 Pixar short "Tin Toy,"

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:48.213
and the eagle from "Toy Story Toons."

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:52.320
So think Mr. Potato Head sounds familiar?

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:54.560
Don Rickles' voice is
actually in the movie

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:57.830
despite the fact that
the actor died in 2017.

00:06:57.830 --> 00:06:59.950
He had signed on to the
movie before his death,

00:06:59.950 --> 00:07:03.120
and his vocal likeness was
compiled through various shorts,

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:06.290
games, and theme-park voiceovers.

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:09.235
And yes, we found A113.

00:07:09.235 --> 00:07:12.370
It's a door number from the
California Institute of Arts,

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:13.560
and it's a number that crops up

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:16.400
in nearly every single Pixar movie.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.050
Also, did you spot Dinoco Gas?

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:20.700
The RV stops there.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:22.380
It's the same chain of gas stations

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:24.290
that Woody and Buzz get stranded at

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:26.490
in the original "Toy Story."

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:28.490
Look closer at the poster for the movie,

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:30.920
and you'll see a reference to "Up."

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:33.210
A painting hangs on the
wall behind the gang

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:34.980
of dogs playing poker,

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:37.130
the same dogs that Russell sailed past

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:38.693
holding a bunch of balloons.

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:41.680
Think you spotted anything that we missed?

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:42.847
Comment below.

